The latest exchanges make it even clearer that no first move by White forces capture. Col5,9 and 14 continue to claim B2 severs the groups and leaves Black short of eyes, yet the concrete replies already enumerated show otherwise: after W B2, B B1 captures the stone outright, joins C1–C2, and leaves the remaining units with the liberty sets A1 B2, A1 A3 B2 and A3 A4 B2. White’s follow-ups at A3 or A4 are then met by B A4 or B B2, restoring the identical A3–B2 eye pair that appears in every other line. The supposed “false-eye” pressure from the C-file wall never materializes because Black already holds two internal points that remain unplayable for White once the capture occurs.  

The same recapture dynamic defeats the other four candidates. Any attempt to list B1, A3 or A4 as viable starters collapses under the immediate Black connection at B2 (or B1) that merges the three units and secures those eyes before White can close a net. Liberty counts confirm the pattern: after each White sacrifice the merged Black shape retains at least three liberties while the eye points stay suicide for both sides. No variation in order or tempo alters that outcome against best defence. The initiating set therefore remains empty.

