The exchanges in round 3 continue to demonstrate that every candidate first move collapses under Black’s immediate reply. Col14’s claim that B2 severs connectivity and creates a decisive liberty shortage is refuted once more by the forced sequence B B1 followed by B B2, which restores the merged A2–B3–B4–C1–C2 shape with liberties A1/A3/A4 and leaves A3–B2 as unplayable internal points. The C3–C4 wall cannot turn A3 into a false eye because Black already holds the tempo to answer any subsequent White play at A3 or A4 with A4 or A1, exactly as the liberty counts enumerated by Cols 2–4, 6–8 and 10–13 show.  

The same recapture dynamic defeats B1: after W B1, B B2 joins the groups and B A1 recaptures, again restoring the A3–B2 eye pair before White can close any net. Attempts at A1, A3 or A4 are even shorter, each met by an instant B1 or B2 connection that either captures the sacrifice outright or secures the two-eye formation. No variation in order alters the outcome against best defence; the five liberty points remain insufficient to force capture. The initiating set therefore remains empty.

